{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1792482356888,"sessionID":"ses_ea7ba165effeGcW5QPif4diEt4","part":{"id":"prt_158462e940010XDKqlKxZsjfJU","messageID":"msg_15846220c001NoLf90ghM5q676","sessionID":"ses_ea7ba165effeGcW5QPif4diEt4","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1792482359463,"sessionID":"ses_ea7ba165effeGcW5QPif4diEt4","part":{"id":"prt_158462e96001ht3bCiLoezQhgz","messageID":"msg_15846220c001NoLf90ghM5q676","sessionID":"ses_ea7ba165effeGcW5QPif4diEt4","type":"text","text":"I've created the Zephyr RTOS application demonstrating priority inheritance with a mutex at `submission/main.c`.\n\nThe application shows how priority inheritance works:\n- The low priority thread (priority 10) acquires the mutex first\n- When the high priority thread (priority 4) tries to acquire the mutex at 100ms, the low priority thread's priority is temporarily boosted to prevent the medium priority thread from preempting it\n- This ensures the low priority thread can finish its critical section and release the mutex quickly\n- The medium priority thread (priority 7) cannot preempt the low priority thread while it holds the mutex that the high priority thread is waiting for","time":{"start":1792482356886,"end":1792482359461}}}
